WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.070
SPEAKER 1: Understanding
workload management in BigQuery

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:06.520
is key to making efficient
use of system resources

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:08.890
and optimizing performance.

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:11.560
In today's episode of
"BigQuery Spotlight,"

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:14.830
we're talking about jobs,
the reservation model,

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:17.590
and best practices for
managing and controlling

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:19.450
work in BigQuery.

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:20.956
So let's get started.

00:00:20.956 --> 00:00:24.288
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:31.890
A job is an action that
BigQuery runs on your behalf.

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:36.810
It can either be a query,
extract, load, or copy job.

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:39.360
When you're performing
actions in the console--

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:43.230
for example, writing a query
against one of your data sets--

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:47.760
BigQuery will go ahead and
create a job on your behalf.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.670
Each job has an
associated user identity

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:54.210
that created the job, a
location or the region

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:56.400
where the job is
actually executed,

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.920
which must be the same
location as the data

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:03.510
that you're using in the job,
and a status, either pending,

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:05.730
running, or done.

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:08.790
Because jobs have the
potential to take a long time

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:12.810
to complete, BigQuery
executes them asynchronously,

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:15.360
meaning each job is
run independently

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.180
and you can execute
multiple jobs at a time

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:20.910
without needing to finish
the current job to move on

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:22.920
to the next one.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:26.220
Since jobs are all doing
some computational work,

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:30.000
each job will need to use
some compute resources.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.540
In BigQuery, a unit of
compute is called a slot,

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:36.330
which is essentially a worker.

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:40.680
A slot is made up of
CPU, RAM, and network.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.230
BigQuery automatically
calculates how many

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:46.380
slots are required by
each job, depending

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:50.520
on the size of the data and
the complexity of the logic.

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:52.680
When BigQuery
executes a query, it

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:55.920
uses the query plan to
execute different aspects

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.420
at different stages.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:00.330
Each stage of the
query may actually

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:03.350
use a different number of slots.

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:05.900
If there isn't a slot
available, BigQuery

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:08.360
may take longer to
finish the query

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.970
as it waits for one to free up.

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:14.330
Before we talk about how
different jobs consume slots,

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:18.290
let's talk about how you
can get access to slots.

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:23.040
As a reminder, BigQuery offers
two different pricing models.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:26.680
First, there's on demand, where
you pay for the amount of bites

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:29.000
scanned in query jobs.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:34.230
In this model, you get 2,000
slots in each BigQuery project.

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:36.680
Alternatively, there's
flat rate pricing,

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:39.140
where you purchase a
number of dedicated slots

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:41.210
for your workloads.

00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:43.160
Flat rate customers
purchase slots

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:46.590
in the form of commitments
that are either annual,

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:52.090
monthly, or flex, which can be
canceled after just 60 seconds.

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:53.680
With the flat rate
model, you can

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:56.050
decide the amount of
slots you need available

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:58.900
for your team's
BigQuery usage, plus

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.930
you have predictable pricing.

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:03.700
Once you have
purchased a commitment,

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:06.010
you can split that
commitment into any number

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:07.960
of reservations.

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:10.870
A reservation is essentially
a bucket of slots

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:14.140
that you can allocate
to specific jobs.

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:17.410
For example, you might
have a BI reservation that

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:20.410
is used for all Business
Intelligence queries

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:23.500
and an ELT reservation
that is used for all Data

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:26.300
Transformation queries.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:28.310
Once you have a
reservation in place,

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:30.680
you can create an
assignment, which

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:34.010
assigns those slots to
your whole organization,

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:38.330
a specific folder, or
even a single project.

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:40.550
Each level in the
resource hierarchy

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:42.380
inherits the assignment
from the level

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:45.960
above, unless you override it.

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:48.420
Now what's really cool
is that idle slots

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:50.370
are available for
other reservations

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:52.930
in your organization to use.

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:54.900
So if no one is
loading a dashboard,

00:03:54.900 --> 00:04:00.280
the slots in your BI reservation
can be used for ELT jobs.

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:02.680
All slots available
to these resources

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:06.250
will be distributed fairly
among projects and then jobs

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:10.530
within those projects, meaning
if there is one query that

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:13.200
is being run in the project,
it will have the ability

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:16.420
to use all the available slots.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:18.760
If there are two queries
that are being run,

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:23.140
then each will have access to
half of the slots, and so on.

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:26.500
So it's unlikely that
one resource-heavy query

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:29.560
will overpower the system
and steal resources

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:32.790
from other running queries.

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:36.090
When you create an assignment,
you specify the type of job

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:38.601
that the assignment
will be used for.

00:04:38.601 --> 00:04:43.400
For example, query assignments
are used for query jobs.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:45.360
Pipeline jobs, on
the other hand,

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:49.220
are used for extract,
copy, and load jobs.

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:53.060
And a quick side note on
this, by default, load, copy,

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:57.200
and export jobs are free and
use a shared pool of slots.

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:00.690
But BigQuery makes no
guarantees on available slots,

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:04.580
which is why you may want to
create a pipeline reservation.

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:07.130
When jobs are assigned
to a pipeline assignment,

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:09.830
they lose access
to that free pool,

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:12.710
so make sure that jobs
have enough capacity.

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:17.350
Otherwise, performance could be
worse than using the free pool.

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:20.800
Finally, there are also
ML_EXTERNAL assignments

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:22.780
for BigQuery machine
learning queries

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:26.010
that use external services.

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:28.130
Now let's consider
how these resources

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:32.510
fit within the broader
BigQuery resource hierarchy.

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:35.120
Commitments, reservations,
and assignments

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:38.330
are created within a
project, sometimes referred

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:41.610
to as the admin project.

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:44.790
Many organizations choose
to purchase all commitments

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:49.130
from a single project to
make it easier to monitor.

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:53.980
These resources also live
within a specific location.

00:05:53.980 --> 00:05:57.020
Commitments will be billed
to the admin project,

00:05:57.020 --> 00:05:58.930
but these slots
are available to be

00:05:58.930 --> 00:06:02.800
used by any jobs within
the organization.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:06.790
Reservations can be also applied
to the whole organization,

00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:10.630
or to any other folder or
project, where they can then

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:15.780
be used by jobs that are
executed in that same location.

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:17.720
This means that you
might have queries

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:21.470
that were run in the
us-northeast-1 region using

00:06:21.470 --> 00:06:24.680
slots that you purchased,
but other queries

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:28.250
in the same project that were
run in the us-multiregion

00:06:28.250 --> 00:06:31.090
might be running on demand.

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:32.830
Now that you have
an understanding

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:37.330
of jobs and the reservation
model, let's see it in action.

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:40.090
Inside the BigQuery console,
I can purchase slots

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:42.010
from the Reservation page.

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:45.640
Here, I will go ahead and
purchase 1,000 flex slots

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:48.580
in the United
States multiregion.

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:50.350
Let's go ahead and confirm that.

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:54.000
Great.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:56.280
Now, once I have
my commitment, I'm

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:59.280
able to create a reservation.

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:01.230
Here, I'll create
a reservation that

00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:05.220
allocates 500 of my slots to
handle queries from my Business

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:07.680
Intelligence tool.

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:10.710
With my reservation in place,
I can create an assignment

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:13.350
that ties this
reservation to my project

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:16.670
where BI queries are run.

00:07:16.670 --> 00:07:18.980
Now, when a query is
run in this project,

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:21.410
it will use slots
in my reservation.

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:27.280
To drill into this further,
I can use the job ID

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:32.050
to select the execution details
from the information schema.

00:07:32.050 --> 00:07:34.450
Here, I can see which
reservation it used

00:07:34.450 --> 00:07:38.080
and how many slots it used on
average throughout the stages

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:39.490
of the query execution.

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:45.240
Look out for an upcoming
video on BigQuery monitoring

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:48.570
where we'll go into detail on
how you can use the information

00:07:48.570 --> 00:07:52.110
schema to understand
utilization and make decisions

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:55.040
around reservation sizes.

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:55.850
Amazing.

00:07:55.850 --> 00:07:59.750
So now you know all about the
BigQuery reservation model.

00:07:59.750 --> 00:08:02.750
Today, we talked about the
standard BigQuery reservation

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:06.560
model, but you may also
hear the term reservation

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:10.700
when digging into BI Engine,
BigQuery's in-memory execution

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:14.600
engine that allows for
super fast queries.

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:17.060
A good way to differentiate
between the two

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:19.610
is that BI Engine
Reservation allocates

00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:23.990
memory for intelligent caching,
while standard reservations

00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:27.080
allocate compute, or slots.

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:29.210
If you want to learn
more about BigQuery,

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:30.920
check out the rest
of our playlist

00:08:30.920 --> 00:08:33.350
and don't forget to
subscribe to our channel

00:08:33.350 --> 00:08:36.200
on YouTube for
other great content.

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:38.990
Look out for our next episode
of "BigQuery Spotlight,"

00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:41.390
and remember, stay curious.

00:08:41.390 --> 00:08:44.740
[MUSIC PLAYING]

